WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.436 --> 00:00:15.079
Dear Abuelita,

00:00:15.103 --> 00:00:17.357
I know I haven't been home for a while.

00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:19.688
You are in our lovely home in Mexico,

00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:22.061
and I'm here in the US,

00:00:22.085 --> 00:00:23.458
fighting for our future.

00:00:23.927 --> 00:00:26.299
You're probably watering the roses,

00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:28.171
taking care of the peaches

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:30.995
and making sure your turtles are well-fed.

00:00:31.474 --> 00:00:34.259
That's one of the things
I miss the most about home --

00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:36.267
spending time with the flowers

00:00:36.291 --> 00:00:38.773
while you tell me stories
about your childhood.

00:00:40.004 --> 00:00:43.988
As you know, we have been living
in New York City since 2015.

00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:47.734
But life has completely changed
over the past year.

00:00:48.243 --> 00:00:51.561
At first, New York City was about museums

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:52.783
and parks

00:00:52.807 --> 00:00:54.487
and school and friends.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.393
Now it serves as a web

00:00:57.417 --> 00:00:59.673
that connects me with all the other people

00:00:59.697 --> 00:01:01.896
who are organizing to save the planet.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.957
You know how it started for me?

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:06.885
It was Dad and his wisdom.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.537
Everything you taught him,
he went and taught the world.

00:01:11.561 --> 00:01:16.450
All of his words about
the responsibility that we have as humans

00:01:16.474 --> 00:01:18.514
to live in balance with nature

00:01:18.538 --> 00:01:20.106
were passed down to me.

00:01:20.585 --> 00:01:23.991
I noticed the universal
disconnect to our planet

00:01:24.015 --> 00:01:26.879
and remembered what you once told me:

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:29.769
"Leave everything better
than you found it."

00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:33.650
I know you were talking about the dishes,

00:01:33.674 --> 00:01:36.447
but of course, that applies
to the planet as well.

00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:38.853
I didn't know what to do at first.

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:40.514
The world is so big,

00:01:40.538 --> 00:01:42.744
and it has so many bad habits.

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:47.085
I didn't know how a 15-year-old
was supposed to change anything,

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:48.402
but I had to try.

00:01:49.181 --> 00:01:51.317
To put this philosophy into practice,

00:01:51.341 --> 00:01:53.895
I joined the environmental club
at my high school.

00:01:53.919 --> 00:01:58.588
However, I noticed that my classmates
were talking about recycling

00:01:58.612 --> 00:02:00.509
and watching movies about the ocean.

00:02:00.947 --> 00:02:03.362
It was a view of environmentalism

00:02:03.386 --> 00:02:07.839
that was so catered towards
an ineffective way of climate activism,

00:02:07.863 --> 00:02:11.279
one that blames the consumer
for the climate crisis

00:02:11.303 --> 00:02:13.839
and preaches that
temperatures are going up

00:02:13.863 --> 00:02:17.293
because we forgot to bring
a reusable bag to the store.

00:02:17.665 --> 00:02:20.744
You taught me that taking care
of Mother Earth

00:02:20.768 --> 00:02:23.888
is about every decision
that we make as a collective.

00:02:25.109 --> 00:02:27.369
I am happy to tell you, Abuelita,

00:02:27.393 --> 00:02:30.364
that I changed everybody's
mind in that club.

00:02:30.388 --> 00:02:32.936
Instead of talking about recycling,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.730
we started to write letters
to our politicians

00:02:35.754 --> 00:02:38.079
to ban soft plastic altogether.

00:02:38.498 --> 00:02:41.266
And then, the unexpected happened:

00:02:41.871 --> 00:02:44.213
we started striking from school.

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:47.848
I know you've probably
seen it on the news,

00:02:47.872 --> 00:02:49.917
and maybe it's not that special anymore.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.622
But at the time,
it was a huge deal, Abuelita.

00:02:53.646 --> 00:02:57.999
Imagine kids not going to school,
because we want people to save the world.

00:02:58.023 --> 00:03:00.964
(Video) Crowd: Another world
is possible! We are unstoppable!

00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:03.380
Xiye Bastida: For the first
global climate strike,

00:03:03.404 --> 00:03:05.282
which was called on by Greta Thunberg,

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:08.958
I got 600 of my classmates
to walk out with me.

00:03:10.474 --> 00:03:13.889
Greta Thunberg is a teenager
who first started striking for climate.

00:03:14.252 --> 00:03:16.642
Her boldness inspired me,

00:03:16.666 --> 00:03:19.555
and I was shocked by the realization

00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:23.598
that youth could turn
public opinion on social issues.

00:03:24.196 --> 00:03:25.347
The movement exploded.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.713
(Video) Crowd: Shut it down!

00:03:26.737 --> 00:03:28.785
XB: And I became
one of the main organizers

00:03:28.809 --> 00:03:31.431
for New York, the US and the world.

00:03:31.455 --> 00:03:33.907
(Video) XB: What do we want?
Crowd: Climate justice!

00:03:33.931 --> 00:03:35.556
XB: When do we want it?
Crowd: Now!

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.880
XB: I started speaking up about
climate justice and Indigenous rights

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.980
and intergenerational cooperation.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.117
That was only the beginning, though.

00:03:43.141 --> 00:03:44.760
The busiest week of my life

00:03:44.784 --> 00:03:48.764
will forever be the week
of September 20, 2019.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:55.595
Me and my friends got 300,000 people
to strike for climate in New York.

00:03:56.023 --> 00:03:57.656
I wish you could have been there.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.613
We walked through Wall Street,
demanding climate justice.

00:04:00.637 --> 00:04:03.925
(Video) Crowd: No more coal, no more oil,
keep the carbon in the soil!

00:04:03.949 --> 00:04:07.045
XB: That same month, I went
to the United Nations Climate Summit.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:09.053
I spoke on a panel with Al Gore.

00:04:09.664 --> 00:04:13.535
I met Jay Inslee and Naomi Klein
and Bill McKibben

00:04:13.559 --> 00:04:16.159
and the president of the United Nations.

00:04:16.742 --> 00:04:19.035
It was the most amazing week of my life,

00:04:19.059 --> 00:04:22.147
because everyone I knew came together --

00:04:22.171 --> 00:04:24.757
all my teachers, all my classmates ...

00:04:24.781 --> 00:04:29.487
And even some of my favorite stores
closed down to strike for climate.

00:04:30.643 --> 00:04:33.180
If you had asked me why I did all of that,

00:04:33.204 --> 00:04:35.142
my only answer would be,

00:04:35.166 --> 00:04:36.606
"How could I not?"

00:04:37.847 --> 00:04:41.172
It's been one year
since it all started for me,

00:04:41.196 --> 00:04:43.195
and it gets a little tiring at times.

00:04:43.688 --> 00:04:47.182
But if there is one thing
that you taught me, it's resilience.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:52.207
I remember that you went to Mexico City
every day for 30 years

00:04:52.231 --> 00:04:54.152
to get money for the family.

00:04:54.176 --> 00:04:58.128
And I know that Abuelito
has been going out for 20 years

00:04:58.152 --> 00:05:01.572
to protect sacred land from big companies
that want to take it.

00:05:02.081 --> 00:05:03.485
A year is nothing

00:05:03.509 --> 00:05:06.437
compared to the struggles
that our family has been through.

00:05:06.855 --> 00:05:10.109
And if our struggles
make the world a better place,

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:12.304
they will make us better people.

00:05:12.673 --> 00:05:15.117
There have been some hardships, Abuelita.

00:05:15.141 --> 00:05:16.547
Out there in the world,

00:05:16.571 --> 00:05:19.807
people expect us kids to know everything,

00:05:19.831 --> 00:05:21.544
or at least they want us to.

00:05:21.568 --> 00:05:24.391
They ask questions and I give answers,

00:05:24.415 --> 00:05:26.950
as if I really knew how the world works.

00:05:26.974 --> 00:05:29.111
They want hope, and we give it.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.903
I have organized, written, spoken
and read about climate and policy

00:05:35.927 --> 00:05:38.260
almost every day for the past year.

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:40.950
And I'm just a little worried

00:05:40.974 --> 00:05:43.365
that I won't be able
to do enough, Abuelita.

00:05:44.484 --> 00:05:48.299
For me, being 18 years old
and trying to save the world

00:05:48.323 --> 00:05:50.228
means being a climate activist.

00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:53.164
Before, maybe it meant
studying to be a doctor

00:05:53.188 --> 00:05:55.482
or a politician or a researcher.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.997
But I can't wait to grow up
and become one of those things.

00:05:59.514 --> 00:06:01.345
The planet is suffering,

00:06:01.369 --> 00:06:03.861
and we don't have the luxury
of time anymore.

00:06:05.469 --> 00:06:09.204
Saving the world as a teenager
means being good with words,

00:06:09.228 --> 00:06:12.093
understanding the science
behind the climate crisis,

00:06:12.117 --> 00:06:15.704
bringing a unique perspective
into the issue to stand out

00:06:15.728 --> 00:06:18.614
and forgetting about
almost everything else.

00:06:19.333 --> 00:06:22.347
But sometimes, I want to care
about other things again.

00:06:22.371 --> 00:06:25.763
I want to be able to sing
and dance and do gymnastics.

00:06:26.214 --> 00:06:30.084
I truly feel that if all of us
took care of the Earth

00:06:30.108 --> 00:06:31.418
as a practice,

00:06:31.442 --> 00:06:33.244
as a culture,

00:06:33.268 --> 00:06:36.611
none of us would have to be
full-time climate activists.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.363
When businesses turn sustainable,

00:06:39.387 --> 00:06:42.625
when the power grid
runs on renewable energy,

00:06:42.649 --> 00:06:45.661
when the school curriculum teaches us

00:06:45.685 --> 00:06:48.919
that taking care of the Earth
is part of our humanity,

00:06:50.008 --> 00:06:52.619
maybe I can do gymnastics once again.

00:06:52.942 --> 00:06:54.441
Don't you think so, Abuelita?

00:06:54.966 --> 00:06:56.116
We can do this.

00:06:56.569 --> 00:06:58.157
All I'm trying to do with my work

00:06:58.181 --> 00:07:01.189
is give that optimistic mindset
to other people.

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:03.425
But it's been a little hard.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.047
There is greed,

00:07:05.071 --> 00:07:06.490
there's pride,

00:07:06.514 --> 00:07:07.766
there's money,

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:09.291
and there's materialism.

00:07:09.315 --> 00:07:12.478
People make it so easy
for me to talk to them,

00:07:12.502 --> 00:07:15.961
but they make it so hard
for me to teach them.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:19.834
I want them to have the confidence
to always do their best.

00:07:20.236 --> 00:07:22.800
I want them to have
the heart and the courage

00:07:22.824 --> 00:07:23.974
to love the world,

00:07:24.768 --> 00:07:26.530
just like you taught me.

00:07:26.554 --> 00:07:28.455
I wrote this letter to thank you.

00:07:28.895 --> 00:07:31.672
Thank you for inviting me
to love the world

00:07:31.696 --> 00:07:33.439
since the moment I was born.

00:07:33.947 --> 00:07:35.836
Thank you for laughing at everything.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:37.348
Thank you for teaching me

00:07:37.372 --> 00:07:41.727
that hope and optimism
are the most powerful tools we have

00:07:41.751 --> 00:07:43.431
to tackle any problem.

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:46.855
I do this work because you showed me

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:48.872
that resilience, love and knowledge

00:07:48.896 --> 00:07:50.624
are enough to make a difference.

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.847
I want to go back to Mexico and visit you.

00:07:53.871 --> 00:07:57.137
I want to show you the pictures
of the things that I have done.

00:07:57.161 --> 00:07:59.482
I want to show you the climate legislation

00:07:59.506 --> 00:08:01.233
that we've been able to pass.

00:08:01.257 --> 00:08:02.964
I want to smell the flowers

00:08:02.988 --> 00:08:05.460
and fight for climate justice
alongside you.

00:08:06.179 --> 00:08:07.361
Te quiero mucho.

00:08:07.385 --> 00:08:08.551
I love you.

00:08:08.575 --> 00:08:09.734
Xiye.

00:08:10.273 --> 00:08:13.777
[Te quiero mucho. Xiye.]

